  "state": {
    "case_id": "73",
    "case_summary": "A 33-year-old Caucasian woman had a lump in her right breast in 1976, which led to a right-sided mastectomy without additional therapy. At age 35, she experienced back pain, and a bone scan showed increased activity in the lumbar spine area. She received local radiotherapy for this finding. After discussing risk-reducing surgery, she opted for a bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy but declined a hysterectomy. Her family history included breast cancer in her mother and maternal grandmother at ages 34 and 41, respectively, with no other known cancers. In 2004, at age 62, genetic testing identified a BRCA1 mutation (185delAG), and she subsequently had a prophylactic left-sided mastectomy. At age 64, she reported changes in bowel habits, faecal leakage, urgency, and left-sided abdominal discomfort. Physical examination found a tender, palpable fullness in the left iliac fossa. Routine blood tests showed haemoglobin at 11.7 g/dl with normal mean cell volume and normal electrolytes, liver, and kidney function. Tumour markers were within normal limits, with carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) at 3 ug/L and CA125 at 3 Ku/L.\n\nBiopsies confirmed a very poorly differentiated papillary adenocarcinoma of the sigmoid, the immunoprofile of which favoured ovarian origin rather than colonic; being highly positive to CK7, while negative to CK20. In addition, there was some faint staining for ER but PR staining was negative. A staging CT scan excluded macroscopic metastases and reported a large bowel-related mass measuring 68 mm by 67 mm, contiguous with the left lateral aspect of the bladder with no associated pathological lymphadenopathy. Laparotomy findings included a bulky tumour adherent to the left pelvic brim and pelvic side-wall, as well as two separate peritoneal deposits, one within the pelvis and one at the terminal ileum. The histology of the surgical specimens revealed peritoneal nodules of poorly differentiated papillary adenocarcinoma, a normal atrophic uterus with no evidence of tumour nor Fallopian tube remnants, and a poorly differentiated papillary adenocarcinoma of the large bowel which penetrated the bowel wall and had invaded adherent bladder. There was associated extramural venous invasion but no metastatic lymph nodes (total of 3 found).\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
